# interplay_dag_abandon_blocked: rvg_device_will_suspend then unpause (an abandoned suspend) while children 2 and 3 are blocked on barriers. rvg_device_will_suspend quiesces every FIFO but, unlike pause and rvg_device_finish_suspend, does not wake the waits (it never signals the FIFOs), so both FIFOs' threads stay in DeviceCore::wait_stamp with their waits registered, and unpause's Fifo::start clears the quiesced flag and starts a second thread on each. The new threads find nothing to read: a child counts what it read locally, and the old thread had read the blocked command. The gate's NOP then wakes the old threads, whose Fifo::barrier_wait now sees the flag clear: they run the held work, which completes in order and matches the control. The new threads are left waiting for a doorbell; the scenario rings neither FIFO again (which thread a doorbell wakes would be timing).
